<section class="how">
    <h2>How to Use This Calculator</h2>
    <p>Start with your trip distance. If you are not sure of the exact mileage, a quick Google Maps search for your route gives a reliable figure. Enter the one-way distance and the calculator also shows you the round trip cost automatically.</p>
    <p>Enter your vehicle's MPG. You can find this on the window sticker of your car, in your owner's manual, or use our Gas Mileage Calculator to measure it accurately from your own driving data. If you are renting a vehicle, the rental company's website usually lists fuel economy for each vehicle class.</p>
    <p>Set the current gas price. Prices vary by state, so use the price near your route for the most accurate estimate. Add tolls and parking costs if you know them. The final number tells you what to budget and exactly how to split the cost if you are traveling with others.</p>
</section>

<section class="formula">
    <h2>How the Road Trip Cost Is Calculated</h2>
    <p><strong>Gallons needed = Distance / MPG</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Fuel cost = Gallons needed x Gas price</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Total trip cost = Fuel cost + Additional costs (tolls, parking)</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Cost per person = Total trip cost / Number of passengers</strong></p>
    <p>For a 500-mile drive at 28 MPG with gas at $3.60 per gallon, you need 17.86 gallons of fuel. That fuel costs $64.29. Add $15 in tolls and the full trip budget is $79.29. Split three ways, each person owes $26.43.</p>
</section>

<section class="examples">
