<section class="faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <h3>How do I find out my car's MPG before a road trip?</h3>
    <p>Fill your tank completely, reset your trip odometer, drive at least 100 miles on a typical mix of roads, then fill up again. Divide the miles on your odometer by the gallons it took to refill. That is your actual real-world MPG for those driving conditions. Use our Gas Mileage Calculator to do this math instantly.</p>

    <h3>Is it cheaper to drive or fly?</h3>
    <p>For trips under 400 to 500 miles, driving is almost always cheaper when you have multiple passengers. The fuel cost gets split while flights are priced per person. For solo travel over 600 miles, budget airlines often undercut the full cost of driving once you factor in depreciation, wear on your vehicle, and any overnight hotel stays needed.</p>

    <h3>Should I use regular or premium gas to save money?</h3>
    <p>Use whatever grade your owner's manual recommends. If your car is designed for regular 87 octane, using premium is a waste of money with no performance or efficiency benefit. If your car requires premium, using regular can cause engine knock and actually reduce fuel economy, costing you more in the long run.</p>

    <h3>How accurate is this calculator?</h3>
    <p>It is as accurate as the numbers you put in. Actual costs can vary due to real-world MPG differences from highway versus city driving, fuel price changes along your route, and driving habits. Building in a 10% buffer over the calculated fuel cost gives you a comfortable safety margin for most trips.</p>
</section>

